      "assistantResponse": "It appears that no files related to the API server configuration or middleware could be found in the current workspace. Let me check if there are any configuration files or directories that may contain relevant information. I'll search for common configuration file types.\nI see that there are only two files in the workspace: `.env.test` and `app.js`. Let me examine the `app.js` file, which is likely the main application file, and the `.env.test` file to understand the API server configuration.\n\nBased on the `app.js` file, I can analyze the API server configuration and identify middleware.\n\nThe application is a simple Express.js server that defines two routes:\n1. `/health` - returns a health check response\n2. `/users` - returns a list of users\n\nAfter reviewing the code, I can see that **there is no explicit middleware configured** in this application.\n\nThe app uses `express()` to create the server instance, but it doesn't register any middleware functions using `app.use()`. In Express.js, middleware are functions that have access to the request and response objects and the next middleware in the application's request-response cycle.\n\nCommon types of middleware that are absent here include:\n- Logging middleware (e.g., morgan)\n- Body parsing middleware (e.g., express.json())\n- Authentication/authorization middleware\n- Error handling middleware\n- CORS middleware\n- Request validation middleware\n\nThe application directly defines the route handlers without any middleware layers in between the incoming requests and the route handlers.\n\nTherefore, this API server has no middleware configured - it directly routes requests to the appropriate handlers without any intermediate processing steps.",
